## 1 · Topic and delivery

| Property | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Topic | `orders.order.confirmed` | `<bounded-context>.<aggregate>.<event>`, per the rule |
| Partition key | `orderId` | Keeps every event of the same order in order |
| Serialization | JSON text — `StringSerializer` / `StringDeserializer` | No cross-team schema contract needed yet — rule default |
| Converted to JSON by | `OrderConfirmedPublisher`, with `JsonMapper`, before `send` | The producer side converts once; under Form B this cell names `OutboxAppender` |
| Converted from JSON by | the `StringJacksonJsonMessageConverter` bean, into the listener's typed parameter | One bean for every listener; the rule's wire contract |
| Delivery | At-least-once + idempotent consumer | Rule default; exactly-once isn't assumed |

## 2 · Producer adapter

Port declared in `10-domain.md`: `PublishOrderConfirmedPort`.

| Property | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Publication form | **A — publish after commit** | `10-domain.md` § Events marks the event `Durability: loss tolerable`: a missed reservation is caught by the next stock reconciliation, so no row survives what a commit already recorded. Form B's table, gateway, and relay would be cost with no failure to prevent — `@.claude/rules/messaging.md` § Publication timing |

| Port | Signature | Adapter | Payload |
|---|---|---|---|
| `PublishOrderConfirmedPort` | `void publish(OrderConfirmed event)` | `OrderConfirmedPublisher` | `OrderConfirmedPayload(orderId, customerId, occurredAt)` |

**Personal data in the payload** — `@.claude/rules/personal-data.md` § In transit. Never omitted:
`none` is the answer when no field qualifies, and an absent line is an unasked question.

| Field | Qualifies as personal data | What crosses | Receiver | Why this form |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `customerId` | no — internal surrogate id, resolves to a person only inside this system | full value | `inventory-service` (this project) | Nothing about the person travels |
| — | none other | — | — | — |

The example that needs the other answer: a field holding a national identifier
(`securityNumber` carrying a CPF) crossing to an external verification service whose whole
purpose is verifying it. That is a legitimate `full value`, and it is recorded here with the
receiver's name — not left to a Javadoc sentence. Masking it for the logs is a different rule
(`@.claude/rules/logging.md`) and does not cover the serialized copy.

Under **Form B** this block carries one more thing, and the partial is incomplete without it:
the relay pass (`RelayOutboxEvents` + `OutboxEventSender` + the application-layer
`OutboxRelayGateway`, shape in `templates/OutboxRelayPublisher.java.example`). Its schedule and
the prune job are not described here either — `jobs-architect` designs both into `35-jobs.md`. The `outbox_events` table it needs is not
described here — it goes as a row of § 6, which is the list `persistence-architect` reads.
One outbox for the whole project, reused by every later event.

## 3 · Consumer adapter and idempotency

| Consuming UC | Listener | Group id | Dedupe key |
|---|---|---|---|
| `ReserveStockUseCase` | `OrderConfirmedListener` | `inventory-service` | `<orderId>:OrderConfirmed` |

Dedupe table: reuses `processed_events`, created by `persistence-architect` for another
consumer in this project. Shared across consumers, not per use case, same pattern as
`idempotency_keys`. Had it not existed yet, it would be a row of § 6 instead.

**Who absorbs the duplicate** — at-least-once is only safe because someone does, so this line
is mandatory even when there is no consumer here:

| Consumer | Where it lives | Idempotency | Evidence |
|---|---|---|---|
| `ReserveStockUseCase` | this project | guaranteed | `processed_events` checked before the use case is called, § 3 above |

When the consumer is **not** in this project, the row says the receiver's name and one of
three words — **contracted** (written down somewhere both sides can point at, and the
`Evidence` column names it), **assumed**, or **unknown** — and the last two are reported as
findings at the end of the run. A guarantee delegated across an organizational boundary with
no contract on this side is a real state of the system; what is not acceptable is it being
invisible.

## 4 · Retry and DLQ

| Decision | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Retry mode | `blocking` | `00-use-case.md` § Concurrency: reservations of one order apply in order, and a later one supersedes an earlier — per-key order decides the outcome (`@.claude/rules/messaging.md` § Blocking or non-blocking) |
| Attempts and backoff | 3 retries, 1 s / 2 s / 4 s | Seconds, well inside `max.poll.interval.ms` |
| DLQ | `orders.order.confirmed.dlq`, source partition, monitored (WARN line + `messaging.dead.lettered{topic,reason}`) | `<topic>.dlq` |
| Shared error handling | REUSE `listener.KafkaListenerErrorHandling` — or NEW on the project's first consumer | Classification is project-wide; this case adds no type to it |

What the project-wide classification does with this case's failures — one row per exception the
consumed use case can raise, read from its `00-use-case.md` error table and `10-domain.md`:

| Failure | Type | Handling |
|---|---|---|
| Stock already reserved for this order with other lines | `ConflictException` (`STOCK_RESERVATION_CONFLICT`) | No retry — straight to the DLQ |
| Another writer changed the stock row first | `ConcurrentUpdateException` (`STOCK_CONCURRENT_UPDATE`) | Retried |
| Database unreachable, lock timeout | not a domain or integration family | Retried, then the DLQ |
| Payload that does not convert | conversion failure | No retry — straight to the DLQ |
| Same order already reserved | none — the use case returns a no-op, logged and counted | Committed, not dead-lettered |

## 5 · Configuration

Values decided for this project. The mandatory ones come from the rule and aren't
re-discussed here — `@.claude/rules/messaging.md` § Configuration.

| Property | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `spring.kafka.consumer.group-id` | `inventory-service` | One stable id per logical consumer |
| `spring.kafka.consumer.auto-offset-reset` | `earliest` | Losing an unprocessed order-confirmed event is worse than reprocessing one |
| `spring.kafka.listener.ack-mode` | not set — Boot's `BATCH` | `reserve()` reaches no other system: redelivering a poll after a crash only re-reads what the dedupe already absorbs. `record` when a consumed use case has an `external HTTP` or `LLM` port, and never lowered once set |
| `spring.kafka.bootstrap-servers` | `${KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS:localhost:${KAFKA_EXTERNAL_PORT:29092}}` | Default has to be the address the HOST resolves — `./mvnw spring-boot:run` runs there. The compose `app` service overrides it with the internal listener, and the port matches the EXTERNAL listener `docker-architect` publishes |

Full shape in `templates/application-kafka.yml.example`.

## 6 · Schema requirements

none

Read by `persistence-architect` in its first pass, which runs after this skill. Every table
or column this transport needs and the domain didn't model goes here, one row each —
requirements, never DDL: the form is that skill's call.

This use case publishes under **Form A**, so nothing survives outside the commit and no
table is needed; the consumer reuses the `processed_events` table that already exists. Under
**Form B**, or with a consumer in a project that has no dedupe table yet, the block is a
table instead:

| Requirement | Why | Columns the design needs | Addressee |
|---|---|---|---|
| `outbox_events` — shared, one per project | Form B: the event is written inside the caller's transaction and published by the relay | event id, aggregate id, topic, payload, occurred at, published at, claim marker, attempts, last error, dead-letter flag | `persistence-architect` step 4b |
| `processed_events` — shared, one per project | Idempotent consumer: the dedupe key is checked before the use case is called | dedupe key, consumed at | `persistence-architect` |

## 7 · Declared dependencies

none

Build dependencies this transport needs and the project does not declare yet, one row each.
The executor applies exactly these and nothing else — `pom.xml` is in its territory only for
a dependency named here (`agent_classes.executor`), so a requirement that never reaches this
block leaves the run stuck at a missing type with nobody entitled to fix it
(lessons-learned-013 § 10).

Version column stays empty whenever the Boot parent manages it: a version written from
memory is what `@CLAUDE.md` invariant 8 forbids.

| Dependency | Version | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-kafka` | (managed by the Boot parent) | Spring Boot 4 moved Kafka autoconfiguration out of `spring-boot-autoconfigure`; without this starter `spring-kafka` alone wires no `KafkaTemplate` and every `spring.kafka.*` property is inert |

## 8 · Personal data

none

Every field of the payload above that matches the derivation in
`@.claude/rules/logging.md` § Masking candidates — the value-object catalog plus the name list.
Run `@.claude/rules/personal-data.md` § How to verify grep 1 against the record this partial designed
and read the hits against that list; `none` is a decision, absence is a gap.

One of three answers per field, and the third column is not optional for the first:

| Field | Form | Receiver and reason |
|---|---|---|
| `securityNumber` (CPF) | full value | The KYC provider verifies the identifier itself: the value **is** the receiver's purpose (`@.claude/rules/personal-data.md` § Admitted exception). Retained by the broker for the topic's retention and by any dead-letter copy — stated here because a message body is kept by whoever consumes it |

`reduced` (an id, a hash, the last digits) and `reference` (the receiver resolves it under its
own authorization) need no receiver column: nothing personal crosses. A **full value** without a
named receiver and a reason is what stops consolidation — *the exception is the record, not the
absence of one*.

## 9 · Deferred

none

One row per item this partial decided **not** to do in this run. `none` is a decision, absence
is a gap: consolidation cannot tell them apart and stops on the second.

Each row names the norm that makes the item mandatory, by path, and the owner it is handed to —
`checklist` (this run implements it) or `backlog` (a later run does, and the spec says what the
project does not guarantee until then). Consolidation resolves the owner: a `checklist` row
becomes a checklist item, a `backlog` row becomes a line in `docs/use-cases/BACKLOG.md` with its
`BL-NN`, which is also what the spec's `## Out of scope` then cites.

| Decided | What is missing | Norm requiring it | Owner |
|---|---|---|---|
| Consumer-side contract test against the external KYC provider | A provider-owned contract to test against — none published yet | `@.claude/rules/messaging.md` § Delivery semantics | `backlog` |

